WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.886 --> 00:00:09.888
&gt;&gt;&gt; Well, at this point, you

00:00:09.888 --> 00:00:13.425
have heard of chatgtp, and it is

00:00:13.558 --> 00:00:14.526
a tool that can write everything

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.828
from research papers and songs,

00:00:16.962 --> 00:00:19.097
and now it has passed the U.S.

00:00:19.231 --> 00:00:20.866
Medical licensing exam.

00:00:20.999 --> 00:00:23.302
It is the latest achievement

00:00:23.435 --> 00:00:25.904
from the artificial intelligence

00:00:25.938 --> 00:00:26.104
tool.

00:00:26.238 --> 00:00:29.908
It had previously passed law

00:00:29.908 --> 00:00:31.910
schools and the entrance exams

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.912
at the Wharton business school.

00:00:33.912 --> 00:00:35.213
So Jacqueline, is this the

00:00:35.347 --> 00:00:37.983
answer to the sophisticated

00:00:38.116 --> 00:00:39.217
medical questions is one thing,

00:00:39.351 --> 00:00:41.186
but how accurate is it really?

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.288
&gt;&gt; Well, it is surprising,

00:00:43.422 --> 00:00:45.924
bianna, but chat gtp got right

00:00:45.924 --> 00:00:46.959
answers more than half of the

00:00:47.092 --> 00:00:48.927
time in the answers that we

00:00:49.061 --> 00:00:50.762
typically use in the U.S.

00:00:50.896 --> 00:00:51.930
Medical licensing exam.

00:00:51.964 --> 00:00:54.800
This is what the study showed

00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:56.535
that the chat bot answered

00:00:56.668 --> 00:00:58.470
questions according to the

00:00:58.603 --> 00:00:59.938
researchers with greater than

00:00:59.938 --> 00:01:01.940
50% accuracy across three tests

00:01:02.074 --> 00:01:04.076
that are used in the licensing

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:05.944
exam, and they said that in most

00:01:05.944 --> 00:01:07.312
cases, it exceeds that it got

00:01:07.446 --> 00:01:10.182
more than 60% accuracy, and that

00:01:10.315 --> 00:01:13.952
60% is what they use to measure

00:01:14.086 --> 00:01:15.320
whether it passed the exam.

00:01:15.454 --> 00:01:17.956
And since it got 60% most of the

00:01:17.956 --> 00:01:19.958
time, it passed or nearly passed

00:01:19.958 --> 00:01:21.493
the U.S. Medical licensing exam.

00:01:21.626 --> 00:01:24.463
Does it mean that chat gdp is

00:01:24.596 --> 00:01:26.365
going to be our doctors in the

00:01:26.498 --> 00:01:26.698
future?

00:01:26.832 --> 00:01:28.300
Probably not.

00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:30.268
Instead, the researchers say it

00:01:30.402 --> 00:01:32.571
could be a reliable tool when it

00:01:32.704 --> 00:01:35.474
comes to making medical

00:01:35.607 --> 00:01:40.379
decisions, diagnoses or plans or

00:01:40.512 --> 00:01:41.980
translating jargon to

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:42.314
understand.

00:01:42.447 --> 00:01:44.583
That is the role we could see.

00:01:44.716 --> 00:01:48.854
&gt;&gt; So no Dr. Chat in the future.

00:01:48.987 --> 00:01:51.023
But it does not feel empathy

00:01:51.156 --> 00:01:54.860
like human doctors, and we have

00:01:54.993 --> 00:01:58.263
heard of medicine becoming more

00:01:58.397 --> 00:01:59.131
personalized, so it could play a

00:01:59.264 --> 00:02:00.332
specific role, but it would not

00:02:00.465 --> 00:02:01.600
take the role of the doctor.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:02.634
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:02:02.768 --> 00:02:04.336
It could be used as a tool, and

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:06.605
while it can make a accurate

00:02:06.738 --> 00:02:08.673
diagnosis, it cannot break down

00:02:08.807 --> 00:02:10.509
how it got there, and how it

00:02:10.642 --> 00:02:12.010
made the decision, and that why

00:02:12.077 --> 00:02:15.380
we are seeing this more so in

00:02:15.514 --> 00:02:17.582
research as a tool for

00:02:18.016 --> 00:02:20.018
translating medical jargon, for

00:02:20.018 --> 00:02:22.120
writing study reports, and for

00:02:22.254 --> 00:02:24.423
drafting pamphlets, bianna, and

00:02:24.556 --> 00:02:26.024
so maybe that is what we will

00:02:26.158 --> 00:02:27.225
see in the future.

00:02:27.359 --> 00:02:28.994
&gt;&gt; So after going to school for

00:02:29.127 --> 00:02:32.030
years on end, we can reassure

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.966
the doctors that they are still

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:35.300
needed.

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:36.134
Jacqueline Howard, thank you.

00:02:36.268 --> 00:02:48.447
&gt;&gt;&gt; As the cchatgtp could improve

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:52.050
and go after your job, we have a

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:53.018
report on what industries are

00:02:53.151 --> 00:02:56.388
already feeling the impact.

00:02:56.521 --> 00:02:58.423
&gt;&gt; Reporter: What jobs are ai

00:02:58.557 --> 00:02:58.924
coming after?

00:02:59.057 --> 00:03:02.060
&gt;&gt; Any kind of middle manager,

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.395
accountants and bookkeepers and

00:03:03.528 --> 00:03:05.063
oddly enough doctors who are

00:03:05.197 --> 00:03:06.998
looking to, who specialize in

00:03:07.132 --> 00:03:08.366
things like drug interactions.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:09.634
&gt;&gt; Do you mean out of the job

00:03:10.068 --> 00:03:11.068
or --

00:03:10.068 --> 00:03:10.535
&gt;&gt; No.

00:03:10.669 --> 00:03:12.070
&gt;&gt; Or that part of your job.

00:03:12.137 --> 00:03:15.240
&gt;&gt; That part of the job.

00:03:15.373 --> 00:03:16.074
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:03:16.074 --> 00:03:16.875
&gt;&gt; Reporter: That is the relief

00:03:17.008 --> 00:03:18.076
that Americans are looking for

00:03:18.076 --> 00:03:21.046
right now, the explosion of chat

00:03:21.179 --> 00:03:22.881
gtp and ai platform showed us

00:03:23.014 --> 00:03:24.716
that we could do a lot of what

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.152
we humans do at work, and

00:03:27.285 --> 00:03:27.486
faster.

00:03:27.619 --> 00:03:28.587
&gt;&gt; Will it take my job?

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:29.387
&gt;&gt; Yes, and no.

00:03:29.521 --> 00:03:31.356
It is not going to replace you,

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.658
and someone who knows how to use

00:03:34.092 --> 00:03:35.060
it well will take your job, and

00:03:35.193 --> 00:03:37.395
that is a guarantee.

00:03:37.529 --> 00:03:40.098
&gt;&gt; By 2024, 8 million jobs are

00:03:40.165 --> 00:03:41.433
going to be displaced by

00:03:41.566 --> 00:03:42.467
automation and technology, but

00:03:42.601 --> 00:03:44.836
it is going to create 97 million

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.104
new roles.

00:03:46.238 --> 00:03:48.573
We have seen it before in the

00:03:48.707 --> 00:03:49.207
auto industry.

00:03:49.341 --> 00:03:52.110
&gt;&gt; While the auto worker may be

00:03:52.177 --> 00:03:55.280
displaced because they are not

00:03:55.413 --> 00:03:57.315
as good as welding or painting

00:03:57.449 --> 00:03:59.184
as a robot, there are 35 people

00:03:59.317 --> 00:04:00.785
who have to be involved in the

00:04:00.919 --> 00:04:02.120
creation and the maintenance of

00:04:02.254 --> 00:04:04.122
that device that welds better

00:04:04.122 --> 00:04:05.056
than a person.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.392
&gt;&gt; That is what happened at

00:04:07.526 --> 00:04:10.262
carbon robotics and the former

00:04:10.395 --> 00:04:12.330
workers are building a laser

00:04:12.464 --> 00:04:14.132
weeder for Detroit farms.

00:04:14.132 --> 00:04:17.269
&gt;&gt; It is a direct result of the

00:04:17.402 --> 00:04:19.037
auto manufacturing that we have

00:04:19.171 --> 00:04:20.572
the skill set available to us

00:04:20.705 --> 00:04:21.673
all in one place.

00:04:21.806 --> 00:04:27.279
&gt;&gt; The laser weeder is operated

00:04:27.412 --> 00:04:32.584
by a robot, but it is run by a

00:04:32.717 --> 00:04:34.686
human, because it fills a role

00:04:34.819 --> 00:04:40.158
that it is harder to find humans

00:04:40.258 --> 00:04:41.359
they cannot find to do the role.

00:04:41.493 --> 00:04:48.533
&gt;&gt; It saves time and money for

00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:51.503
roles that humans are hard to

00:04:51.636 --> 00:04:51.803
find.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:54.239
&gt;&gt; Ai is more affordable, and

00:04:54.372 --> 00:05:00.512
there is no composer or musical

00:05:00.645 --> 00:05:00.845
artist.

00:05:00.979 --> 00:05:08.520
&gt;&gt; It is taking food away from

00:05:08.653 --> 00:05:10.922
families because artists are not

00:05:11.056 --> 00:05:12.791
able to fulfill it.

00:05:12.924 --> 00:05:14.192
&gt;&gt; Reporter: And Ortiz claims

00:05:14.192 --> 00:05:15.794
that they are using her name and

00:05:16.194 --> 00:05:17.829
art to train the ai.

00:05:18.196 --> 00:05:20.198
&gt;&gt; It is feast and famine for

00:05:20.298 --> 00:05:22.300
most of us.

00:05:22.434 --> 00:05:25.804
We go job by job, and what

00:05:26.204 --> 00:05:27.205
happens when there is a little

00:05:27.339 --> 00:05:29.507
bit less work to go around.

00:05:29.641 --> 00:05:32.210
&gt;&gt; Stability ai as it is called

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:33.411
says the student misunderstands

00:05:33.545 --> 00:05:35.680
how the ai and copyright law

00:05:35.814 --> 00:05:37.482
works and says that it intends

00:05:37.616 --> 00:05:39.818
to defend ourselves and the

00:05:40.218 --> 00:05:41.853
potential that ai has to expand

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:45.490
the creative power of humanity,

00:05:45.624 --> 00:05:51.696
and the other two companies

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.164
failed to respond.

00:05:53.298 --> 00:05:59.304
&gt;&gt; It is straight out of the

00:05:59.437 --> 00:05:59.638
movie.

00:05:59.771 --> 00:06:04.242
&gt;&gt; And can the robot write a

00:06:04.309 --> 00:06:05.877
symphony, can the robot turn the

00:06:06.244 --> 00:06:08.346
canvas into a masterpiece.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.248
&gt;&gt; And the robot looks up, and

00:06:10.315 --> 00:06:11.616
he says, can you?

